Higher‑dimensional extensions of General Relativity, notably Kaluza–Klein‑type models, provide a geometric unification of gravitation and electromagnetism. When the velocity space of the four‑dimensional spacetime is endowed with a nonholonomic distribution, the resulting constraints modify the geodesic flow and can generate effective gauge fields without invoking additional compactified dimensions. Understanding how a charged test particle moves under these combined geometric structures is essential for assessing the physical viability of such models. Starting from a five‑dimensional pseudo‑Riemannian manifold \((\mathcal{M}^{5},g_{AB})\) with the metric split \(g_{AB}=  egin{pmatrix} g_{\mu
u}+ \phi^{2}A_{\mu}A_{
u} & \phi^{2}A_{\mu}\ \phi^{2}A_{
u} & \phi^{2}\end{pmatrix}\), we imposed a four‑dimensional nonholonomic constraint on the particle’s four‑velocity \(v^{\mu}\) defined by a linear Pfaffian form \(\omega_{\mu}(x)v^{\mu}=0\). The equations of motion were derived via the Lagrange–d’Alembert principle, incorporating the constraint through Lagrange multipliers, and subsequently projected onto the physical spacetime using the orthogonal decomposition associated with the distribution. Symbolic computation verified the consistency of the resulting system, and a set of numerical integrations illustrated typical trajectories. The derived equations exhibit the standard Lorentz force term together with an additional “extra‑dimensional” force proportional to the gradient of the scalar field \(\phi\) and to the curvature of the nonholonomic distribution. Conservation of the generalized momentum is retained, while the usual mass‑shell condition is modified by a term quadratic in the constraint one‑form. These demonstrate that a nonholonomic velocity space naturally generates electromagnetic‑like interactions and predicts observable deviations from geodesic motion, offering a testable signature of five‑dimensional General Relativity models.

The rationality of the elliptic Gauss sum coefficient is shown. The following is a specific case of our argument. Let f(u)=sl((1-i)\varpi u), where sl() is the Gauss' lemniscatic sine and \varpi=2.62205... is the real period of the elliptic curve y^2=x^3-x, so that f(u) is an elliptic function relative to the period lattice Z[i]. Let \pi be a primary prime of Z[i] such that norm(\pi)\equiv 13\mod 16. Let S be the quarter set mod \pi consisting of quartic residues. Let us define G(\pi):=\sum_{
u\in S} f(
u/\pi) and 	ilde{\pi}:=\prod_{
u\in S} f(
u/\pi). The former G(\pi) is a typical example of elliptic Gauss sum; the latter is regarded as a canonical 4-th root of -\pi: (	ilde{\pi})^4=-\pi. Then we have Theorem: G(\pi)/(	ilde{\pi})^3 is a rational odd integer. G(\pi) appears naturally in the central value of Hecke L associated to the quartic residue character mod \pi, and our proof is based on the functional equation of L and an explicit formula of the root number. In fact, the latter is nothing but the Cassels-Matthews formula on the quartic Gauss sum.
